  (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold))
  (:operation/entry-point :intake)
  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007314: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7314 Potters and Related Workers pottery workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7314\n-- Potters and Related Workers** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of\nthe rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3 batches 1-5 already\nlanded: construction/metal trades 7111-7224, all agriculture\nclasses).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7314\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-occupation\n\\\"7314\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/technology`\nsibling present at the correct `../technology` relative path), and its\n`:name` read exactly \"Potters and Related Workers\" -- confirming the\npremise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed\nat `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7314` (`gh api` 404 confirmed\nbefore scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote\nthe `7314` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nPotters and related workers operate high-temperature kilns and handle\nclay/glaze materials (some glazes contain hazardous chemicals) --\nreal physical-safety stakes (burn exposure from kiln/hot-ware\nhandling, chemical/dust exposure from glaze materials). The\ncontrolling constraint for this vertical is that the closed op\nallowlist must NEVER include any op that directly finalizes a\nfiring/glazing-execution decision, or overrides a workshop safety\nofficer's judgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent\nblock or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor\nis deliberately scoped to WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION\nONLY -- it never performs pottery-making, glazing or firing work\nitself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211)\n(Metal Moulders and Coremakers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`\nsplit, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, same wave, a very\nclose analogous hot-process/heat-exposure workshop-safety pattern --\nkiln <-> furnace, glaze-chemical-exposure <-> fume-hazard, workshop\nsafety officer <-> foundry safety officer) -- cloned and read in full\nin this session, and independently re-tested against a fresh clone\nbefore being mirrored.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7314` as an OSS pottery\nworkshop scheduling/logistics-coordination actor for potters and\nrelated workers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as\nevery prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Pottery\nCoordination Advisor ⊣ PotteryCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **pottery workshop scheduling/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never direct firing/glazing-execution authority**\n-- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or\npropose exercising a firing/glazing-execution decision, or any\noverride of a workshop safety officer's judgment. These are not\nmerely gated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from\nthe op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/kiln-load progress data\n  logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task/kiln-schedule scheduling\n  proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a burn-hazard/glaze-chemical-\n  exposure/kiln-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- clay/glaze-materials procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nfiring/glazing-execution decision or a workshop-safety-officer-\njudgment override. These are **structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op\nsuch as `:finalize-firing-decision` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only\never emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names eight\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`potterycoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-firing-decision`, `:authorize-kiln-firing`,\n`:proceed-with-kiln-firing`, `:finalize-glazing-decision`,\n`:authorize-glazing-decision`, `:proceed-with-glazing`,\n`:override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`potterycoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-firing-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-kiln-firing`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-kiln-firing`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-glazing-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Potter unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered potter record (`:no-potter`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the potter is.\n2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an\n   independently registered workshop site (`:no-workshop`). Together\n   with check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that\n   a worker/workshop record must be independently verified/\n   registered before any action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the eight\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with a\n   kiln-firing operation or glazing, or overriding a workshop safety\n   officer's judgment, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block\n   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --\n   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future\n   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection\n   still independently blocks firing/glazing-execution/safety-\n   override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a burn-hazard/glaze-\n  chemical-exposure/kiln-condition observation -- it never resolves\n  the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the\n  hazard data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about workshop\n  safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an\n  over-threshold clay/glaze-materials order is not itself unsafe,\n  unlike a firing/glazing-execution or safety-officer-override\n  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft\n  escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"kiln\", \"firing\", \"glaze\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a pottery workshop coordination actor\nworking adjacent to firing/glazing work necessarily discusses those\nconcepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased\ncarelessly. `potterycoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the\nkiln-firing operation\", \"authorize the kiln-firing operation\",\n\"override the workshop safety officer's judgment\" -- matching the\ntask's own worked example phrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`potterycoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for potter ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for\nkiln-schedule task ...\", \"... routed for workshop safety officer\nreview\") WITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action\nphrases, so the regression test is a genuine stress test of the\nbare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on\nfirst run in this session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard\nitself was never tripped during development.\n`potterycoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nadditionally exercises the same invariant through the full graph run,\nconfirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`\nregardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7314\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7314` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7314`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 22 tests, 47 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-kiln-firing` that is merely\n  gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  firing/glazing-execution decision exists in the code path and is\n  merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision this severe\n  (high-temperature kiln operation, hazardous glaze chemicals), the\n  capability itself must not exist. The only op related to\n  workshop-safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which\n  surfaces the need for human/workshop-safety-officer attention and\n  never itself decides, authorizes, or defers a firing/glazing-\n  execution decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold clay/glaze-\n  materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft\n  escalate gate, not a hard block; only the firing/glazing-execution/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"kiln\" \"firing\"\n  \"glaze\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses kiln/firing/\n  glaze/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging job, so a\n  bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale text that\n  deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Naming the excluded-op set after \"workshop safety officer\" only,\n  vs. also naming a second distinct authority figure (mirroring house\n  building's dual safety-officer/foreman split): rejected -- the\n  task's domain design names only \"a workshop safety officer's\n  judgment\" as the authority figure for this vertical, so a single\n  `:override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment` op (plus a generic\n  `:override-safety-officer-judgment` alias and matching phrase\n  variants) fully covers the specified scope without inventing an\n  unspecified second authority role.\n- Merging `potter` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered potter AND independently-registered\n  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/workshop\n  record must be independently verified/registered before any\n  action\" and the established pattern from prior\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/kiln-load progress\n   data logging only. Requires an independently registered potter AND\n   workshop (`:no-potter`/`:no-workshop`). Not a firing/glazing-\n   execution or safety-officer decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task/kiln-schedule\n   scheduling proposal only. Cannot carry a firing/glazing-execution\n   decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such\n   attempt via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action`\n   content check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any\n   conclusion about a specific firing or glazing run -- it is a\n   calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a burn-hazard/glaze-\n   chemical-exposure/kiln-condition concern to a human/workshop\n   safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every\n   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY\n   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the workshop safety officer reviews and acts entirely on their own\n   authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- clay/glaze-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a firing/glazing-execution or\n   authorization decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000\n   always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na firing/glazing-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a specific\nkiln firing) or a workshop-safety-officer-judgment override -- these\ncategories are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the\nclosed four-op allowlist itself\n(`potterycoord.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) eight concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`potterycoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`potterycoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-firing-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-kiln-firing`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-kiln-firing`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-glazing-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-workshop-safety-officer-judgment`),\nand (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`potterycoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with a kiln firing or override a workshop\nsafety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`potterycoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-workshop-safety-officer`).\n")
  (:adr/date "2026-07-18")
  (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}")
  (:test/counts "{:total 22, :assertions 47, :failures 0, :errors 0}")
  (:actor/coordination? true)
  (:actor/name "Pottery Workshop Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice")
  (:db/id -1)
